---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ImageTool from '../../components/tools/ImageTool.tsx';
---
<Base title="Resize Image to 20 KB | FormFix India" description="Resize a photo or scan to 20 KB (20,480 bytes) or less as JPEG, in your browser. Preview before and after, with the actual output size verified.">
<nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol class="flex gap-2 text-sm text-slate-500"><li><a class="underline" href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-hidden="true">/</li><li aria-current="page">Resize Image to 20 KB</li></ol></nav>
<h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold mt-4">Resize Image to 20 KB</h1>
<p class="text-slate-600 mt-2 max-w-2xl">For forms that ask for a photograph or document image no larger than 20 KB. The tool tries the highest quality first, then smaller sizes, and measures every candidate by actual bytes.</p>
<div class="mt-6"><ImageTool client:load targetKiB={20} title="Resize Image to 20 KB" /></div>
<section class="mt-10"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Instructions and limits</h2><p class="mt-2 text-slate-700">20 KB is a tight limit. Detailed photos may need a smaller dimension or lower quality to fit, and may look soft. If the tool cannot reach 20 KB at acceptable quality, it will say so rather than claim success. Try a simpler scan, crop out empty background first, or check whether your form accepts a larger size.</p><p class="mt-2">Related: <a class="underline" href="/tools/image-to-50kb/">Image to 50 KB</a> · <a class="underline" href="/tools/signature-10-to-20kb/">Signature 10–20 KB</a> · <a class="underline" href="/application-checker/">Application checker</a></p></section>
<section class="mt-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Questions</h2><div class="space-y-4 mt-4"><div class="card p-5"><h3 class="font-bold">Will my image stay the same shape?</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-700 mt-1">Yes, aspect ratio is preserved unless you crop in the signature tool. The image is not stretched.</p></div><div class="card p-5"><h3 class="font-bold">Why is my PNG output a JPEG?</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-700 mt-1">JPEG compresses photos far better at small sizes. Transparent areas become white, because JPEG has no transparency.</p></div></div></section>
</Base>
